#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcad847-55c5-54c9-978e-aab990d0c5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183846} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e420be-7458-5309-8fa6-53d19346dcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183847} {:answer/id #uuid "226bcbbc-cfb5-56a0-801f-07bdeefada77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183848} {:answer/id #uuid "343b2896-72d4-5135-9e03-fd07955dee6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183849} {:answer/id #uuid "70bd5fc4-279e-5b7a-a253-acc7b5e9554c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16439" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343b2896-72d4-5135-9e03-fd07955dee6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183849} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc495eb-3490-5a68-b1d6-e8a03d5ba5b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa3dd46a-8d87-5019-a6c8-488757b3b77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9608" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places."}]}
